00:06:17.393 --> 00:06:18.124
<v Bill Scott>And they don't forget it.

00:06:18.124 --> 00:06:24.175
<v Mike Hall>Yeah. And you're right. I hadn't even thought about that before this interview. But about the, there's the constant story on Reddit about the, my, this charity or my Kickstarter got frozen. I don't know why.

00:06:24.175 --> 00:06:49.374
<v Bill Scott>Yep. And what we do is what, David does this, I do this, a bunch of us do this. We're watching Reddit. We're watching Hackney News. We're watching Twitter. As soon as you see something like that, we go fix the problem. But then what we do is we look under the cover saying, okay, what's the policy change in this thing? Sometimes these things are overnight. You know, for example, I had, I forget who it was exactly, but I had, you know, one of the conference organizers, you know, all of a sudden you got this account's frozen line. And we went to work on it and, you know, it was obvious that, look, this is a common occurrence. You can't just say if somebody's all of a sudden money starts coming in really, really fast, that that's a bad thing, you know, because there are certain scenarios where that works. So the risk team has to develop different models.

00:06:49.374 --> 00:06:49.871
<v Mike Hall>New models, yeah.

00:06:49.871 --> 00:06:54.870
<v Bill Scott>Just think about it. You got 120 million customers. You know, it's not like somebody sitting in and going, I'm going to freeze this account. It's just computer algorithms.

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:55.454
<v Mike Hall>Right. That's right.

00:06:55.454 --> 00:06:59.547
<v Bill Scott>So what happens you have, if you're, you know, the call center support team is not enabled to fix those problems, then you double whammy it.

00:06:59.547 --> 00:06:59.722
<v Mike Hall>Right.

00:06:59.722 --> 00:07:06.066
<v Bill Scott>So it's just stuck in the mind. So David did things like, you know, the team actually that does the call center support, some of them, they actually enable them with what they call tokens, almost like goodwill tokens.

00:07:06.066 --> 00:07:06.241
<v Mike Hall>Right.

00:07:06.241 --> 00:07:13.286
<v Bill Scott>Or basically if they can take one of those tokens and fix any problem and can't get fired, can't get in trouble. If they make a mistake, you learn from mistakes. So you have a certain amount of these tokens you can kind of utilize to get on.

00:07:13.286 --> 00:07:15.625
<v Mike Hall>That's interesting. It's like, you're going to enable, we're going to trust you.

00:07:15.625 --> 00:07:15.771
<v Bill Scott>Yeah.

00:07:15.771 --> 00:07:16.560
<v Mike Hall>Just do what you got to do.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:17.788
<v Bill Scott>Yeah. But we're going to have a safeguard.

00:07:17.788 --> 00:07:19.893
<v Mike Hall>Yeah. And there's a little bit of spending there you have to do with it.

00:07:19.893 --> 00:07:22.144
<v Bill Scott>Yeah. You know, so you have to make a decision. You know, you think about it.

00:07:22.144 --> 00:07:22.465
<v Mike Hall>Yeah. Yeah.

00:07:22.465 --> 00:07:43.396
<v Bill Scott>Yeah. You know. So those kind of things have changed. And we actually, you know, I don't know the exact number, but it's a pretty high number each month. Less account reasons. Less account goals. Funny story. I'll give you a funny story. So, early this year, there's a story came on Hacker News about this kid who created a JavaScript library, an animation library. And he was a junior in college, I actually, in Northern Kentucky University. And PayPal had locked his account because he made a tremendous, I mean, I found a tremendous amount of money in a very short period of time. And he got on Hacker News and said, I think he said something like, you know, I made about $200,000. And PayPal's locked my account.

00:07:43.396 --> 00:07:43.542
<v Mike Hall>Yeah.

00:07:43.542 --> 00:07:45.588
<v Bill Scott>So we saw it. We went to work, unlocked his account, and we hired him.

00:07:45.588 --> 00:07:45.910
<v Mike Hall>Oh, really?

00:07:45.910 --> 00:07:48.219
<v Bill Scott>He came in and did amazing stuff for us, one of the best interns I've ever had.

00:07:48.219 --> 00:07:48.365
<v Mike Hall>Yeah.

00:07:48.365 --> 00:08:01.549
<v Bill Scott>And he's coming back. We made him my offer. He's coming back next year and joining us. Had a blast. We were about to do so much articles about that. And I really loved doing that because, you know, PayPal has been, I gave a talk in Ireland last week at Node Congress. And one of the guys was writing up stories about it. He said, he called my talk mesmerizing. Because, he said, all of us in the crowd had written PayPal off as being in the dark ages.

00:08:01.549 --> 00:08:01.725
<v Mike Hall>Right.

00:08:01.725 --> 00:08:35.839
<v Bill Scott>And it was so refreshing because, you know, we're leading the charge on Node. We're one of the companies that's actually, we're about to open source a bunch of Node frameworks. But, you know, we brought a bunch of people from the outside, from other companies. We also had some great people inside that were already there. And we changed. I kind of like that story. That's a funny story for me is, you know, how do I get engineering and design work together? How do I get technology? And frankly, I'm having no trouble recruiting at all. I'm getting some really great people. Because, you know, people, at the end of the day, engineers and designers, whoever, what you're going to work on is really important. So we got that. Who are you going to work with? So I brought some great people in from lots of good companies. That's important. And how you're going to work, that's this lean UX, lean style of working, is critical. If you get the who, how, and the what down right, of course, you got to pay decent. Maybe good location helps. But those are all, you know, they're not the main thing. Right? People make all kinds of sacrifices to get the how, who, and what.

00:08:35.839 --> 00:08:41.715
<v Mike Hall>Well, I mean, it sounds like one of the things when you hear about a story like the young man who made $200,000 off his library is he's got some decent seed money that he could spend on the next thing.

